WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.980
If you look at cars from the 1970s and cars
from the 1990s, there’s really one big difference

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.290
in their designs.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.530
The ones from the 70s are boxy, and the ones
from the 90s are curvy.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:15.200
For some car models, you can even tell the
exact year when that change happened.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.999
Just look at this commercial for the Buick
LeSabre, showing the 1991 model — and then

00:00:20.999 --> 00:00:23.849
the 1992 model.

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:24.849
See the curves?

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:26.380
Let’s watch that again.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.750
‘91 is boxy, sharp edges.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:29.750
‘92?

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.499
Smooth and round.

00:00:31.499 --> 00:00:34.460
Since then, cars have become curvier and curvier.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.100
So how did this happen?

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.050
When manufacturers started making curvy cars
in the 90s, it wasn’t a totally new concept.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:47.879
Back in the 1930s, streamliners like this Chrysler
Airflow used this sleek design to reduce wind

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:49.000
resistance.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.539
But as the 50s and 60s rolled around, American
streamliners stopped selling well — they

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:56.500
were outsold by bigger, boxier cars.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.820
Gas prices were on fairly steady decline at
this point, so streamlining for fuel efficiency

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.129
was less of a concern.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:08.140
Well into the 1970s, just about every car
made in America had sharp edges and very few

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.140
curves.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.220
They were basically designed as a series of
three boxes — the hood, the cabin, and the

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.220
trunk.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.380
That model worked really well in the US market.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.979
But in Europe, fuel was always more expensive.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:25.619
In the 60s, a gallon of gas in France cost
a whopping 73.1 cents while it was just 31

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:26.950
cents in the US.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.329
So European designers started experimenting
with more aerodynamic designs to help cars

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:34.409
move more easily so they’d waste less gas.

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:38.960
Automakers like Porsche, BMW, Audi, Mercedes-Benz
— they all started rolling out car models

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.950
that had curved exteriors.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.250
Eventually American automakers started to
copy the European aerodynamic look to try

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.310
to attract upscale consumers.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.740
The mass market was a different picture.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.149
In Europe, a designer named Uwe Bahnsen was
the first person to push for a curved design

00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:58.420
for the average driver, and he did that with
the 1982 Ford Sierra.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.470
It was curvier than any car in its class at
the time.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.390
But critics just laughed at it. And it didn't sell very well at first.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.729
It was nicknamed the “jelly mould” because
of how much it looked like the kind of circular

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.069
shape you’d make JELL-O with.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.500
But one of the designers from that European
Ford design team — this guy, Jack Telnack

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.770
— took over the US design team in 1980.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.790
American gas prices at this point were skyrocketing
due to conflict in the middle east.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:25.510
So Telnack brought wind tunnel testing into
the design process.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.250
He first did that with the 1983 Ford Thunderbird.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.720
But the real breakthrough came a few years
later:

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.680
"The result was the groundbreaking 1986 Ford
Taurus."

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.370
It might not look that groundbreaking now,
but this style was revolutionary

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.370
at the time.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.380
It was a mass market car with curvy edges
— and people liked it.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.380
“Taurus!

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:53.300
Now a North American car with a shape and
a feel we’ve never seen before…

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:54.300
Taurus for us!”

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.550
They used it in RoboCop as the car of the
future.

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:05.099
The sales basically saved Ford, which had
really been struggling at the time, and it inspired

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:07.830
a whole wave of copycat curvy cars.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:11.860
And streamlining became even more popular
because manufacturers were facing new fuel

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.989
economy regulations in the US.

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:17.849
Starting in 1978, the average fuel efficiency
of each manufacturer’s passenger cars had

00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:19.569
to meet higher and higher levels.

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:23.489
According to one Ford designer, aerodynamic
design was a much more affordable way to boost

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:26.260
efficiency than doing engineering work under
the hood.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.709
The rise of computer modelling in the 80s
also made it easier for car makers to design

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.519
and manufacture curved lines.

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:36.300
All these years later, the influence on today’s
cars is pretty clear.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.830
Even the models that we criticize for being
ridiculously boxy these days — like the

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.000
Scion xB — are actually really round.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.379
And if our modern day sci fi movies have anything
to say about it, that won’t be changing

00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:48.659
anytime soon.

